A Hybrid Control Theory for Tyre-Free Ground-Level Permanent-Magnet Electrodynamic Suspension


Ouadi Maakoul and SI TEAM 

Abstract

This thesis develops a mathematically closed hybrid control theory for a tyre-free road vehicle supported during cruise entirely by ground-level permanent-magnet electrodynamic suspension (PMEDS), with a retractable auxiliary gear providing standstill, low-speed, and emergency support. The principal theoretical contributions are: (i) a rigorous derivation of the reduced-order Inductrack force laws with explicit averaging error bounds; (ii) a stability analysis of the lagged electrodynamic suspension model that corrects the quasi-static equilibrium assumption and yields a certified minimum active-damping bound; (iii) a regularised load-fraction formulation with a well-posed transition-rate constraint; (iv) a hierarchy of safety-critical controllers—exponential and input-to-state-safe control barrier functions, Clarke-gradient nonsmooth barrier functions, and high-order barrier functions—each justified at its correct relative degree; (v) a discriminating-kernel formulation of fault tolerance that accounts for disturbance and gear-failure uncertainty; (vi) a Hunt–Crossley contact model for the auxiliary gear replacing the ill-posed linear spring-damper model; and (vii) a trajectory-reachability formulation of deployment timing replacing the algebraic constraint of earlier revisions. The reference vehicle has mass m=500 kg, gap band [15,25] mm, and maximum downward active authority exceeding 7 kN. The resulting architecture is falsifiable, stability-certified, and ready for numerical construction of the hybrid feasible domain, the viability kernel, and the robust capture basin.

---

1. Introduction

1.1 From Overhead Rail to Tyre-Free Ground-Level Magnetic Support

Attractive electromagnetic suspension (EMS) requires an overhead ferromagnetic rail and is therefore a guideway architecture. Ground-level magnetic support must push the vehicle upward from below, which requires repulsive electrodynamic suspension (EDS). The architecture considered in this thesis is:

\text{vehicle body} \;\downarrow\; \text{Halbach array} \;\downarrow\; \text{conductive magnetic road}

with a retractable auxiliary gear that is deployed only during standstill, low speed, and emergency landing. During cruise, the gear is fully retracted, no mechanical contact exists between vehicle and road, and the vehicle is supported by PMEDS plus a bidirectional active suspension.

Removing pneumatic tyres eliminates rolling resistance, tyre wear, tyre mass, and tyre compliance uncertainty, but it also removes the only passive vertical suspension element between body and road. The gap becomes the sole vertical degree of freedom. The active suspension must therefore possess bidirectional authority — it must pull the vehicle down when passive lift exceeds weight, not merely push it up. This is a non-optional structural requirement of the tyre-free architecture, and it drives every control-theoretic statement in this thesis.

1.2 Research Questions

The thesis answers five questions:

1. Under what conditions do the reduced-order Inductrack force laws constitute a rigorous continuous-time dynamical model?
2. Is the quasi-static equilibrium of the lagged EDS model stable, and if not, what active damping is required?
3. How are load fraction, force balance, and transition rate to be defined so that the corresponding safety constraints are well-posed?
4. Which control barrier function class is correct at which relative degree for each constraint, and how are the resulting quadratic programs guaranteed feasible?
5. Under what enlarged disturbance and fault set is the operating set contained in the robust capture basin of the gear-supported set?

1.3 Thesis Contributions

1. Rigorous Inductrack derivation with averaging error bounds and transient time-scale quantification (Chapter 2).
2. Stability certification of the lagged EDS model, proving that the quasi-static centre is unstable and deriving a minimal active-damping bound (Chapter 3).
3. Regularised load-transfer dynamics with well-posed transition rate and continuous gear state (Chapter 4).
4. Hunt–Crossley gear model replacing the ill-posed linear spring-damper contact (Chapter 5).
5. Correct-relative-degree safety controllers: exponential CBFs, high-order CBFs, input-to-state-safe CBFs, and Clarke-gradient nonsmooth CBFs, with a compatibility lemma for the QP (Chapter 6).
6. Discriminating-kernel fault tolerance with quantified fault timing and gear-failure inclusion (Chapter 7).
7. Hybrid feasible domain with corrected active authority, continuous gear state, and mode-guarded deployment timing (Chapter 8).
8. Hybrid corridor graph and optimal routing (Chapter 9).

1.4 Structure

Chapter 2 derives the EDS force laws and quantifies the domain of validity. Chapter 3 analyses the lagged model and proves the minimal damping bound. Chapter 4 formalises load transfer. Chapter 5 develops the gear contact model. Chapter 6 constructs the safety controllers. Chapter 7 develops the viability and discriminating-kernel theory. Chapter 8 constructs the hybrid feasible domain. Chapter 9 extends to the corridor network. Chapter 10 concludes and lists open problems.

