2. Electrodynamic Suspension Force Laws

2.1 Circuit Dynamics

Consider a single shorted track circuit of inductance L and resistance R moving relatively through a Halbach field of wavelength \lambda and wavenumber k=2\pi/\lambda. The magnetic flux linking the circuit is

\phi(t)=\phi_0\cos(\omega t),\qquad \omega=kv,

where v is the vehicle speed and \phi_0 depends on the gap g and Halbach geometry. The circuit equation is

L\frac{dI}{dt}+RI=\omega\phi_0\cos(\omega t).

This is a linear time-invariant first-order ODE driven by a sinusoid. Its solution consists of a transient decaying as e^{-(R/L)t} and a unique T=2\pi/\omega-periodic steady state. The transient decay time is

\tau_e=\frac{L}{R}.

Lemma 2.1 (Transient time scale). For every v>0, the circuit current converges to its unique T-periodic steady state with time constant \tau_e=L/R. The relative error after time t is O(e^{-t/\tau_e}).

For the reference track (L=2\,\mu\text{H}, R=0.1\,\text{m}\Omega), \tau_e=20 ms. This is not negligible compared to the mechanical time scales of the vehicle, and must be carried explicitly in the stability analysis of Chapter 3.

2.2 Steady-State Current and Averaged Forces

The periodic steady-state current is

I(t)=\frac{\phi_0}{\sqrt{R^2+(\omega L)^2}}\cos(\omega t-\varphi),\qquad \tan\varphi=\frac{\omega L}{R}.

The instantaneous Lorentz force on the circuit has a mean component and oscillatory components. The wavelength-averaged lift and drag per circuit are

\bar F_y=\frac{k\phi_0^2}{2L}\cdot\frac{(\omega L/R)^2}{1+(\omega L/R)^2},\qquad
\bar F_x=\bar F_y\cdot\frac{R}{\omega L}.

Substituting \omega=kv and defining the transition velocity

v_t=\frac{R}{kL},

yields

\bar F_y=\frac{k\phi_0^2}{2L}\cdot\frac{v^2}{v^2+v_t^2},\qquad
\bar F_x=\bar F_y\cdot\frac{v_t}{v}.

2.3 Halbach Peak Field and Flux

The peak surface field of an M-block-per-wavelength Halbach array of thickness d and remanent field B_r is

B_0=B_r\bigl(1-e^{-kd}\bigr)\frac{\sin(\pi/M)}{\pi/M}.

The flux linked by a track circuit of width w at gap g is

\phi_0(g)=\frac{B_0w}{k}e^{-kg}.

2.4 Averaged Force Laws

Substituting and averaging over the array area A_H=wL_H, the lift and drag are

\boxed{F_M(v,g)=\frac{F_\infty(g)}{1+(v_t/v)^2}},\qquad
\boxed{F_D(v,g)=F_M(v,g)\frac{v_t}{v}},

with

\boxed{F_\infty(g)=\frac{B_0^2w^2L_H}{2kLd_c}e^{-2kg}}.

The dissipated power is

P_D(v,g)=F_D(v,g)v=F_\infty(g)v_t\frac{v^2}{v^2+v_t^2},

which is monotonically increasing in v and saturates at P_D^\infty=F_\infty v_t.

2.5 Averaging Error Bound

The passage from instantaneous force to wavelength-averaged force must be justified if the continuous-time model is to be used in a control-theoretic proof.

Lemma 2.2 (Averaging error). Let N_H=L_H/\lambda be the number of wavelengths spanned by the Halbach array. Then the difference between the instantaneous vertical force and its wavelength average is bounded by

\bigl|F_y(t)-\bar F_y\bigr|\le C\frac{\bar F_y}{N_H},

for a constant C of order unity depending only on the Halbach geometry.

Proof sketch. The instantaneous force is a trigonometric polynomial in 2\omega t whose amplitude is proportional to \bar F_y. Integrating over N_H wavelengths averages the oscillatory part to order 1/N_H, with the leading correction arising from the boundary terms of the array. The constant C is bounded by the L^1 norm of the Fourier coefficients.

Consequence. The continuous-time model F_M(v,g) is exact in the limit N_H\to\infty and has relative error O(1/N_H). For the reference parameters N_H=6, so the error is a few percent—acceptable for the reduced-order control model but must be documented.

2.6 Gap Derivative Error

Differentiating F_M(v,g) with respect to g yields

\frac{\partial \ln F_M}{\partial g}=-2k+\frac{2k}{1+(v/v_t)^2}\cdot\frac{v_t^2}{v^2}= -2k+\frac{2k v_t^2}{v^2+v_t^2}.

So the exact derivative is

\frac{\partial \ln F_M}{\partial g}=-2k\cdot\frac{v^2}{v^2+v_t^2}.

The leading term -2k is exact only in the high-speed limit v\gg v_t. All ECBF gains depending on -2k must be computed from the exact expression.

2.7 Asymptotic Convergence Rate

The high-speed limit satisfies

\bigl|F_M(v,g)-F_\infty(g)\bigr|\le F_\infty(g)\Bigl(\frac{v_t}{v}\Bigr)^2.

This bound justifies the choice of v_{\rm rob} as the speed at which F_M has approached F_\infty to within the required margin.

