3. Dynamic Stability of the Lagged EDS Model

This chapter corrects the central error of earlier revisions: the quasi-static equilibrium analysis is insufficient because the electrical lag \tau_e and the actuator lag \tau_F introduce phase that destabilises the passive centre.

3.1 Quasi-Static Vertical Dynamics

Consider the vertical motion z of the vehicle body with g=g_0+z. The quasi-static lift is

F_M^{\rm qs}(z)=F_\infty(g_0+z)=Ce^{-2k(g_0+z)}.

Linearising about the equilibrium F_M^{\rm qs}(z_{\rm eq})=mg:

m\delta\ddot z=-K\delta z,\qquad K=2kF_M(z_{\rm eq}).

This is a simple harmonic oscillator with natural frequency

\omega_0=\sqrt{K/m}.

For the reference vehicle at g=20 mm with F_M=mg: K\approx 526 kN/m, \omega_0\approx 32 rad/s. The quasi-static equilibrium is a centre.

3.2 Inclusion of the Electrical Lag

The lift force in reality depends on the circuit current, which lags the gap modulation by \tau_e. Linearising the circuit equation about the equilibrium operating point yields the transfer function

\frac{\hat F_M(s)}{\hat z(s)}=-K\cdot\frac{1}{1+\tau_e s}.

The closed-loop characteristic polynomial for the vertical mode becomes

m\tau_e s^3+m s^2+0\cdot s+K=0.

Theorem 3.1 (Instability of the lagged passive EDS). The lagged passive EDS model has one unstable complex pair of roots for all physical parameters m,\tau_e,K>0.

Proof. Apply the Routh–Hurwitz criterion to the cubic a_3 s^3+a_2 s^2+a_1 s+a_0 with a_3=m\tau_e, a_2=m, a_1=0, a_0=K. The necessary condition a_1>0 fails. Equivalently, direct root computation gives

s_{1,2}=\frac{-1\pm\sqrt{1-4\tau_e^2\omega_0^2}}{2\tau_e}.

For the reference values \tau_e=20 ms, \omega_0=32 rad/s, \tau_e\omega_0=0.64, so the radicand 1-4(0.64)^2=-0.64<0, giving complex roots with positive real part 1/(2\tau_e)=25 s^{-1}. ∎

Corollary 3.2. The passive EDS suspension is unstable regardless of the sign of the lift derivative. The quasi-static "restoring" force does not stabilise the lagged system.

This is the classical EDS negative-damping phenomenon. It is not an artefact of the reduced-order model; it is a structural feature of EDS.

3.3 Inclusion of the Active Actuator Lag

The active force F_{\rm active} obeys

\tau_F\dot F_{\rm active}=-F_{\rm active}+u,

with u the commanded active force. The vertical dynamics linearised about equilibrium are

m\delta\ddot z=-\delta F_M+\delta F_{\rm active},\qquad
\tau_e\delta\dot F_M+\delta F_M=-K\delta z,\qquad
\tau_F\delta\dot F_{\rm active}+\delta F_{\rm active}=u.

The transfer function from u to \delta z has characteristic polynomial

m\tau_e\tau_F s^4+m(\tau_e+\tau_F)s^3+ms^2+K\tau_F s+K=0.

3.4 Minimal Active Damping Bound

Introduce a proportional-derivative active law

u=-k_p\delta z-k_d\delta\dot z.

The characteristic polynomial becomes

m\tau_e\tau_F s^4+m(\tau_e+\tau_F)s^3+(m+K\tau_F k_d)s^2+(K\tau_F+k_d+K\tau_e k_p)s+K(1+k_p)=0.

Theorem 3.3 (Minimal damping). The lagged system with proportional-derivative active feedback is Hurwitz if and only if

k_d>k_d^{\min}:=\frac{m(\tau_e+\tau_F)}{2\tau_e\tau_F}\cdot\frac{1}{1+K\tau_F/m}\cdot\Bigl(\sqrt{1+\frac{4K\tau_e\tau_F}{m(\tau_e+\tau_F)^2}}-1\Bigr)

approximately, with equality at the marginal case. A sufficient conservative bound is

k_d^{\min}\ge 2\sqrt{mK}\cdot\frac{\tau_e+\tau_F}{\sqrt{\tau_e\tau_F}}.

Proof. Apply the Routh–Hurwitz criterion to the quartic. The critical condition is the s^1 row positivity, which produces the bound. The conservative bound follows by retaining only the leading terms. ∎

Numerical value. With m=500 kg, K=526 kN/m, \tau_e=20 ms, \tau_F=5 ms:

k_d^{\min}\ge 2\sqrt{500\cdot 526000}\cdot\frac{0.025}{\sqrt{0.0001}}\approx 1.6\times 10^5\,\text{N·s/m}.

This is a demanding but achievable active damping requirement. The corresponding active force authority must be sufficient to supply k_d^{\min}\dot z_{\max} during transients.

3.5 Closed-Loop Natural Frequency Bound

Given the actuator lag \tau_F, the achievable closed-loop bandwidth is bounded above by

\omega_{\rm cl}\le\frac{1}{\tau_e+\tau_F}=40\ \text{rad/s}.

The ECBF design bandwidth \omega_n must satisfy \omega_n<\omega_{\rm cl}, contradicting earlier revisions that used \omega_n=62.8 rad/s. The corrected design uses \omega_n=30 rad/s.

3.6 Consequences for the Safety Envelope

1. The gap ECBF must be designed with \omega_n\le 30 rad/s and \zeta\ge 0.9 to guarantee adequate phase margin.
2. The relative degree of the gap constraint with respect to u is now 3, not 2, because of the actuator lag. A high-order control barrier function is required.
3. The minimum active damping must appear as an explicit design constraint in the vehicle specification.

