4. Hybrid Load Transfer Dynamics

4.1 Notation Correction

Let F_{\rm PMEDS}(v,g) denote the passive lift produced by the Halbach-track interaction. Let F_{\rm active} denote the commanded active force. The total magnetic force is

F_M=F_{\rm PMEDS}+F_{\rm active}.

The load fraction is defined with respect to the total magnetic force and the gear contact force:

\boxed{\lambda=\frac{F_M}{F_M+F_G}}.

The force balance is

m\ddot z=F_M+F_G-mg+d_z=F_{\rm PMEDS}+F_{\rm active}+F_G-mg+d_z.

Define

\boxed{\epsilon_F=F_{\rm PMEDS}+F_{\rm active}+F_G-mg},

so that m\ddot z=\epsilon_F+d_z. The two quantities \lambda and \epsilon_F carry independent information.

4.2 Regularisation at F_M+F_G=0

The definition of \lambda is undefined when F_M+F_G=0. A regularised load fraction is introduced:

\lambda_\varepsilon=\frac{F_M}{F_M+F_G+\varepsilon},\qquad \varepsilon>0.

The regularisation is exact in the sense

\lim_{\varepsilon\to 0}\lambda_\varepsilon=\begin{cases}1,&F_G=0,\ F_M>0,\\ 0,&F_M=0,\ F_G>0,\\ \frac{F_M}{F_M+F_G},&\text{otherwise.}\end{cases}

For the transition-rate constraint, the regularised form is used everywhere, with \varepsilon chosen smaller than the smallest measurable force in the system.

4.3 True Transition Rate

Differentiating the regularised load fraction:

\dot\lambda_\varepsilon=\frac{(F_G+\varepsilon)\dot F_M-F_M\dot F_G}{(F_M+F_G+\varepsilon)^2}.

In the limit \varepsilon\to 0:

\boxed{\dot\lambda=\frac{F_G\dot F_M-F_M\dot F_G}{(F_M+F_G)^2}},\qquad F_M+F_G>0.

The components are

\dot F_M=\frac{\partial F_{\rm PMEDS}}{\partial v}\dot v+\frac{\partial F_{\rm PMEDS}}{\partial g}\dot g+\dot F_{\rm active},

\dot F_G=\frac{\partial F_G}{\partial z_{\rm rel}}\dot z_{\rm rel}+\frac{\partial F_G}{\partial \dot z_{\rm rel}}\ddot z_{\rm rel}+\dot\sigma\cdot(\cdots).

4.4 Non-Convexity and Linearisation

The set \{\dot\lambda\le\dot\lambda_{\max}\} is non-convex in the control variables (\dot F_{\rm active},\dot F_G) because the constraint is quadratic. A quadratic-program formulation with a non-convex constraint is not automatically solvable.

Resolution. The constraint is replaced by two conservative linear inequalities obtained from a first-order Taylor expansion about the current state. Let a=F_G+\varepsilon, b=F_M, c=(F_M+F_G+\varepsilon)^2. The function

\dot\lambda_\varepsilon=\frac{a\dot b-b\dot a}{c}

is linear in (\dot a,\dot b)=(\dot F_G,\dot F_M). Hence, at each instant, the constraint \dot\lambda_\varepsilon\le\dot\lambda_{\max} is linear in the control derivatives. The non-convexity appears only if one attempts to write the constraint in terms of the underlying physical controls (\dot v,\dot g,\dot F_{\rm active},\dot z_{\rm rel}). By treating (\dot F_{\rm active},\dot F_G) as the effective control variables at the level of the transition-rate constraint, the linearity is recovered.

Lemma 4.1 (Linear transition-rate constraint). At each state with F_M+F_G+\varepsilon>0, the regularised transition-rate constraint is equivalent to the linear inequality

(F_G+\varepsilon)\dot F_M-F_M\dot F_G\le\dot\lambda_{\max}(F_M+F_G+\varepsilon)^2.

The QP feasibility under box constraints on \dot F_{\rm active} and \dot F_G follows if the box constraints admit a strictly feasible point of the linear inequality.

4.5 Equilibrium Manifolds

Passive (no active, no gear):

\mathcal E_{\rm passive}=\{(v,g):F_{\rm PMEDS}(v,g)=mg\}.

Controlled (active present, no gear):

\mathcal E_{\rm hybrid}=\{(v,g,F_{\rm active}):F_{\rm PMEDS}(v,g)+F_{\rm active}=mg\}.

Gear-supported (standstill):

\mathcal E_{\rm gear}=\{F_G=mg,\ v\approx 0\}.

4.6 Four-Mode Hybrid Automaton

Mode \lambda Support Entry guard
0 — Gear \approx 0 Gear v<v_{\rm disengage} or no corridor
1 — Transition (0,1) Gear + magnetic v\ge v_{\rm engage}, corridor, F_M+F_{\rm active}\ge mg
2 — Magnetic cruise 1 Magnetic only v\ge v_{\rm rob}, gap in band, \sigma=0
3 — Emergency \downarrow 0 Gear Fault, v\downarrow v_{\rm disengage}, or gear-fail-to-retract

Hysteresis: v_{\rm engage}>v_{\rm disengage}.

