6. Safety-Critical Control

6.1 Combined Safe Set

\mathcal K=\mathcal K_{\rm gap}\cap\mathcal K_{\rm gear}\cap\mathcal K_{\rm vel}\cap\mathcal K_{\rm act}\cap\mathcal K_{\rm power}\cap\mathcal K_{\lambda}\cap\mathcal K_{\rm deploy},

with each component defined below.

6.2 Gap Constraint: High-Order CBF

With the actuator lag included, the gap constraint has relative degree 3 with respect to the commanded force u, because the chain is

u\to F_{\rm active}\ (\tau_F)\to \ddot g\to \dot g\to g.

The correct barrier function is a high-order CBF with a triple integrator filter:

h_3=\ddot h+K_2\dot h+K_1 h+K_0\int h\,dt\ge 0,

or equivalently, using the HOCBF formulation with r=3:

\boxed{h^{(3)}(x,u)+k_2 h^{(2)}(x)+k_1 h^{(1)}(x)+k_0 h(x)\ge 0.}

The gains (k_0,k_1,k_2) are chosen such that the polynomial s^3+k_2 s^2+k_1 s+k_0 is Hurwitz, with bandwidth strictly less than 1/(\tau_e+\tau_F).

Design values. With \omega_n=30 rad/s and \zeta=0.9: k_2=3\zeta\omega_n=81, k_1=3\omega_n^2+3\zeta^2\omega_n^2\approx 4700, k_0=\omega_n^3=27000.

The HOCBF guarantees forward invariance of \{h\ge 0\} provided the constraint is feasible on the actuator-admissible set.

6.3 Gear-Load Constraint: Clarke CBF

The gear constraint

h_G=F_G-F_{G,\min}\ge 0

is Lipschitz but not C^1. It is enforced via a Clarke CBF:

\dot h_G+\alpha(h_G)\ge 0,\qquad \xi\in\partial_C h_G(x),

which reduces to a linear inequality in the commanded force at each instant because F_G is affine in \sigma and \sigma is affine in the commanded gear force.

6.4 Power Constraint: ISSf-CBF

The power constraint

h_D=P_{\max}-P_D(v,g)\ge 0

is relative degree 1 in the combined input u=(u_{\rm thrust},u_{\rm active}). In the presence of disturbance d_z, the standard CBF is replaced by an input-to-state-safe CBF:

\boxed{\dot h_D+\alpha(h_D)\ge \iota(\|d_z\|)},

where \iota is a class-\mathcal K function. The set \{h_D\ge 0\} is then rendered ISS-safe: states remain within a neighbourhood of the safe set whose size shrinks to zero as the disturbance vanishes.

6.5 Transition-Rate Constraint: Linear Affine Constraint

As shown in Section 4.4, the regularised transition-rate constraint is linear in the control derivatives (\dot F_{\rm active},\dot F_G). It is therefore an affine input constraint, not a barrier function. It is added to the QP as a linear inequality.

6.6 Deployment-Timing Constraint: Mode-Guarded Reachability

The algebraic constraint of earlier revisions

h_{\rm dep}=v-v_{\rm phys}^{\sup}-a_{\rm decel}t_{\rm deploy}\ge 0

is incorrect for two reasons:

1. It reduces to a constant floor v\ge v_{\rm phys}^{\sup}+a_{\rm decel}t_{\rm deploy} that must hold in all modes, contradicting Mode 0 (gear-supported, v may be zero).
2. It treats a_{\rm decel} as a free parameter, whereas in fact a_{\rm decel} is determined by magnetic drag and thrust.

The correct formulation is a mode-guarded trajectory-reachability constraint. When the transition to Mode 0 is contemplated (i.e., \sigma_{\rm cmd} is about to be commanded to 1), the reachability constraint is:

\boxed{\forall t\in[t_0,t_0+T_{\rm horizon}],\quad v(t)\ge v_{\rm phys}^{\sup}\ \text{OR}\ \sigma(t)\ge\sigma_{\rm loaded},}

where T_{\rm horizon} is the deployment horizon and \sigma_{\rm loaded} is the gear-state threshold at which the gear can sustain mg without magnetic support. The trajectory v(t) is generated by integrating

m\dot v=-F_D(v)-F_{\rm prop}^{\rm brake}(t)+\text{thrust},

and the constraint is enforced only when \sigma_{\rm cmd} transitions from 0 to 1. In Mode 0 and Mode 3, the constraint is inactive.

6.7 Quadratic Program with Guaranteed Feasibility

The QP is

\min_{u}\ \|u-u_{\rm nom}\|^2

subject to:

· HOCBF gap (Section 6.2),
· Clarke CBF gear (Section 6.3),
· ISSf-CBF power (Section 6.4),
· linear affine transition-rate constraint (Section 6.5),
· reachability constraint when \sigma_{\rm cmd} transitions (Section 6.6),
· actuator box constraints.

Lemma 6.1 (Feasibility compatibility). If the nominal input u_{\rm nom} satisfies all constraints with positive slack and the box constraints have non-empty interior, then the QP is feasible in a neighbourhood of the nominal trajectory.

Proof. By continuity of the constraint functions and the strict feasibility of u_{\rm nom}, there exists \epsilon>0 such that u_{\rm nom}+\delta u remains feasible for \|\delta u\|<\epsilon. ∎

6.8 Relative-Degree Summary

Constraint Relative degree Framework
Gap g\in[g_{\min},g_{\max}] 3 (with \tau_F) HOCBF
Gear load F_G\ge F_{G,\min} 1 (nonsmooth) Clarke CBF
Power P_D\le P_{\max} 1 (with d_z) ISSf-CBF
Transition rate \|\dot\lambda\|\le\dot\lambda_{\max} 0 (algebraic in \dot F) Linear affine
Deployment timing mode-dependent Reachability

