7. Viability, Discriminating Kernel, and Fault Tolerance

7.1 Viability Kernel

The viability kernel of \mathcal K under control u and disturbance d is

\operatorname{Viab}(\mathcal K)=\{x_0\in\mathcal K:\exists u(\cdot),\forall d(\cdot),\forall t\ge 0,\ x(t)\in\mathcal K\}.

7.2 Discriminating Kernel

The standard viability kernel does not account for the ability of the disturbance to push the state out of the safe set. The discriminating kernel does:

\boxed{
\operatorname{Disc}(\mathcal K,\mathcal C)=\{x_0\in\mathcal K:\exists u(\cdot),\forall d(\cdot),\exists T\ge 0,\ x(T)\in\mathcal C\ \text{and}\ x(t)\in\mathcal K\ \forall t\in[0,T]\}.
}

The discriminating kernel is the correct object for fault tolerance in the presence of disturbance and gear-failure uncertainty.

7.3 Fault Model

The admissible fault set \mathcal F_{\rm admissible} contains:

· Disturbance: d_z\in[-d_{\max},d_{\max}], with d_{\max}\approx 0.2mg.
· Gear-fail-to-deploy: \sigma remains at 0 despite \sigma_{\rm cmd}=1, occurring at an unknown time t_f.
· Active-actuator loss: F_{\rm active} decays to zero with time constant \tau_{\rm fail}.
· Magnetic-loss: F_{\rm PMEDS} drops by a factor \rho\in(0,1].

7.4 Fault-Timing Quantification

The gear-failure fault is parametrised by its occurrence time t_f. The discriminating kernel must be computed for the worst-case t_f:

\boxed{
\operatorname{Disc}_{\rm worst}(\mathcal K,\mathcal C_{\rm gear})=\bigcap_{t_f\ge 0}\operatorname{Disc}(\mathcal K,\mathcal C_{\rm gear};t_f).
}

The thesis's admissibility claim is

\boxed{\mathcal K_{\rm operating}\subseteq\operatorname{Disc}_{\rm worst}(\mathcal K,\mathcal C_{\rm gear}).}

7.5 Hybrid Discriminating Kernel

The state includes the continuous variables (z,\dot z,\theta,\dot\theta,\sigma) and the discrete mode q\in\{0,1,2,3\}. The hybrid discriminating kernel is defined on the hybrid state space. Its existence requires:

1. Lipschitz continuity of the continuous vector fields.
2. Closeness of \mathcal K and \mathcal C_{\rm gear}.
3. Tangential condition on the boundary of \mathcal K under the worst-case disturbance.
4. Well-defined jump map for mode transitions.

Under these hypotheses, the hybrid discriminating kernel is closed and non-empty provided a viable hybrid feedback exists.

7.6 Saint-Pierre Approximation

The Saint-Pierre algorithm computes an approximation from outside for viability kernels and from inside for discriminating kernels. The direction of the approximation must be stated for safety:

· Viability kernel: outer approximation is conservative for existence of control.
· Discriminating kernel: inner approximation is conservative for safety (smaller set is safer).

For the thesis, the inner approximation of the discriminating kernel is used, because the safety claim is that all operating states can be driven to the gear-supported set despite faults.

7.7 Grid Dimensionality Correction

Earlier revisions computed "50^4\times 2" grid points, treating \sigma as binary. With \sigma\in[0,1] continuous, the state dimension is 5 and the grid size is

N_{\rm grid}=50^5\approx 3.1\times 10^8,

which is infeasible for direct computation. Resolution: use adaptive mesh refinement or Hamilton–Jacobi with level sets to obtain a certified inner approximation with a stated accuracy.

7.8 CLF Sufficient Condition

If there exists V(x) and an invariant \Omega\subseteq\mathcal K with

\dot V\le-\alpha V+\beta\|d\|,

and \Omega\subseteq\operatorname{Disc}(\mathcal K,\mathcal C_{\rm gear}), then admissibility holds on \Omega. The CLF provides a certificate in cases where direct kernel computation is intractable.

