9. Corridor Network and Hybrid Optimal Control

9.1 Hybrid Graph

G=(V,E_0\cup E_M)

with E_0 ordinary asphalt edges (\sigma=1, \lambda=0) and E_M magnetic-corridor edges (transition permitted).

9.2 Edge Transitions

· On E_0: Mode 0 or Mode 3.
· On E_M with v\ge v_{\rm engage}: Mode 0 → 1 → 2.
· On E_M with v<v_{\rm engage}: Mode 0 or partial Mode 1.
· On exit to E_0: Mode 2 → 1 → 0 or Mode 2 → 3.
· Fault on E_M: Mode 2 → 3 → \mathcal C_{\rm gear}.

9.3 Hybrid Optimal Control

The route planning problem is

\min_{P=(e_1,\dots,e_n)}\ \int_P\bigl(P_{\rm prop}+P_D+P_{\rm gear}+\gamma T_{\rm transition}\bigr)\,dt,

subject to:

· vehicle dynamics (Chapter 3),
· mode guards (Section 4.6),
· safety constraints (Chapter 6),
· discriminating-kernel membership (Chapter 7).

The coupling with the discriminating kernel is essential: the route planner must not select edges whose engagement points lie outside the discriminating kernel.

9.4 Deployment-Timing Reachability

For each candidate engagement edge e_M, the planner integrates the trajectory

m\dot v=-F_D(v)-F_{\rm prop}^{\rm brake}(t)+\text{thrust}

from the disengagement point backwards in time, verifying that \sigma reaches \sigma_{\rm loaded} before v drops below v_{\rm phys}^{\sup}. If not, the edge is infeasible.

