1. The transient time scale \tau_e=20 ms is comparable to the mechanical bandwidth. A full time-varying analysis of the coupled electrical-mechanical system is required beyond the linearised model.
2. The non-convexity of the transition-rate constraint is resolved by linearisation, but the linearisation introduces conservatism. A less conservative treatment using sum-of-squares programming is an open direction.
3. The discriminating kernel under gear-failure uncertainty requires fault-timing quantification; the worst-case over all fault times is computationally demanding.
4. The Hunt–Crossley model requires calibration of the nonlinear exponent n and the coefficient of restitution e for the specific gear material.
