Appendix A: Full Derivation of the Inductrack Force Laws

A.1 Circuit Dynamics with Faraday's Law

A single shorted track circuit of inductance L and resistance R links a time-varying flux from the moving Halbach array. In the track frame, the vertical field at the circuit plane is

B_y(x,t)=B_0\cos\!\big(k(x-vt)\big)e^{-kg},

where g is the gap and k=2\pi/\lambda. The flux through a circuit of width w is

\phi(t)=\phi_0(g)\cos(\omega t),\qquad \omega=kv,\qquad \phi_0(g)=\frac{B_0w}{k}e^{-kg}.

By Faraday's law the induced EMF is -\dot\phi=\omega\phi_0\sin(\omega t), so

L\dot I+RI=\omega\phi_0\sin(\omega t).

A.2 Steady-State Solution

The unique 2\pi/\omega-periodic solution has the form I(t)=A\cos\omega t+B\sin\omega t. Substituting:

(-\omega LA+RB)\sin\omega t+(\omega LB+RA)\cos\omega t=\omega\phi_0\sin\omega t.

Matching coefficients:

-\omega LA+RB=\omega\phi_0,\qquad \omega LB+RA=0.

Solving:

A=-\frac{L\omega^2\phi_0}{R^2+L^2\omega^2},\qquad B=\frac{\omega R\phi_0}{R^2+L^2\omega^2}.

The amplitude is |I|_{\max}=\omega\phi_0/\sqrt{R^2+L^2\omega^2}, and the phase lags by \arctan(\omega L/R). In the high-speed limit \omega L\gg R the current becomes inductive, I\to(\phi_0/L)\sin\omega t.

A.3 Instantaneous Lorentz Force

The vertical and horizontal forces on one circuit are

F_y(t)=I(t)\frac{\partial\phi}{\partial y}=-k\phi_0 I(t)\cos\omega t,

F_x(t)=I(t)\frac{\partial\phi}{\partial x}=-k\phi_0 I(t)\sin\omega t.

Substituting I=A\cos\omega t+B\sin\omega t and averaging over one period:

\langle F_y\rangle=-\frac{k\phi_0 A}{2},\qquad \langle F_x\rangle=-\frac{k\phi_0 B}{2}.

A.4 Wavelength-Averaged Force Laws

Substituting A,B:

\langle F_y\rangle=\frac{kL\omega^2\phi_0^2}{2(R^2+L^2\omega^2)},\qquad
\langle F_x\rangle=-\frac{k\omega R\phi_0^2}{2(R^2+L^2\omega^2)}.

Introducing the transition velocity v_t=R/(kL) and using \omega=kv:

\langle F_y\rangle=\frac{k\phi_0^2}{2L}\cdot\frac{v^2}{v^2+v_t^2},\qquad
\langle F_x\rangle=-\frac{k\phi_0^2}{2L}\cdot\frac{v v_t}{v^2+v_t^2}.

Hence \langle F_y\rangle/\langle F_x\rangle=-v/v_t, so the lift-to-drag ratio is v/v_t.

A.5 Array Aggregation

The Halbach array has area A_H=wL_H and N_H=L_H/\lambda wavelengths. With 1/d_c circuits per unit length, the total number of contributing circuits is N=L_H/d_c. The high-speed per-area lift is

\frac{F_\infty}{A_H}=\frac{B_0^2 w}{2kLd_c}e^{-2kg},

and multiplying by A_H=wL_H:

\boxed{F_\infty(g)=\frac{B_0^2 w^2 L_H}{2kLd_c}e^{-2kg}}.

The lift and drag then satisfy

F_M(v,g)=\frac{F_\infty(g)}{1+(v_t/v)^2},\qquad F_D(v,g)=F_M(v,g)\frac{v_t}{v}.

A.6 Power Dissipation

P_D=F_D v=F_\infty(g)v_t\frac{v^2}{v^2+v_t^2}.

Differentiating:

\frac{dP_D}{dv}=F_\infty v_t\frac{2vv_t^2}{(v^2+v_t^2)^2}>0,

and \lim_{v\to\infty}P_D=F_\infty v_t=P_D^\infty. Thus P_D is strictly increasing and saturates.

