Appendix E: Well-Posedness of the Regularised Load Fraction

E.1 Definition and Limit

Define for \varepsilon>0

\lambda_\varepsilon(x)=\frac{F_M}{F_M+F_G+\varepsilon}.

The map is continuous and 0\le\lambda_\varepsilon<1.

E.2 Limit as \varepsilon\to 0^+

If F_G>0: \lambda_\varepsilon\to F_M/(F_M+F_G).
If F_G=0 and F_M>0: \lambda_\varepsilon\to F_M/(F_M+\varepsilon)\to 1.
If F_M=0 and F_G>0: \lambda_\varepsilon\to 0.

So the limit is well-defined and finite except when both F_M=F_G=0, which is not a physically realizable state (weight must be supported).

E.3 Transition Rate Under Regularisation

\dot\lambda_\varepsilon=\frac{(F_G+\varepsilon)\dot F_M-F_M\dot F_G}{(F_M+F_G+\varepsilon)^2}.

The numerator is affine in (\dot F_M,\dot F_G). The denominator is strictly positive. Hence the constraint |\dot\lambda_\varepsilon|\le\dot\lambda_{\max} is equivalent to two linear inequalities

(F_G+\varepsilon)\dot F_M-F_M\dot F_G\le \dot\lambda_{\max}(F_M+F_G+\varepsilon)^2,

(F_G+\varepsilon)\dot F_M-F_M\dot F_G\ge -\dot\lambda_{\max}(F_M+F_G+\varepsilon)^2,

at each state. ∎

---

Appendix F: Linearity of the Transition-Rate Constraint (Lemma 4.1)

At each state (F_M,F_G), the quantities a=F_G+\varepsilon and b=F_M are fixed. The constraint \dot\lambda_\varepsilon\le\dot\lambda_{\max} becomes

a\,\dot F_M-b\,\dot F_G\le \dot\lambda_{\max}(a+b)^2.

Both \dot F_M and \dot F_G are affine functions of the physical controls:

\dot F_M=\frac{\partial F_{\rm PMEDS}}{\partial v}\dot v+\frac{\partial F_{\rm PMEDS}}{\partial g}\dot g+\dot F_{\rm active},

\dot F_G=\dot\sigma f_G^{\rm HC}+\sigma\frac{\partial f_G^{\rm HC}}{\partial z_{\rm rel}}\dot z_{\rm rel}+\sigma\frac{\partial f_G^{\rm HC}}{\partial \dot z_{\rm rel}}\ddot z_{\rm rel}.

Introducing the effective control w=(\dot F_{\rm active},\dot\sigma,\dot v,\dot z_{\rm rel},\ddot z_{\rm rel})^T, the inequality a\dot F_M-b\dot F_G\le c is linear in w. Hence the constraint is an affine input constraint. ∎

---

Appendix G: High-Order Control Barrier Function Invariance (Appendix to Section 6.2)

G.1 Definition (Nguyen–Sreenath)

Let h:\mathbb R^n\to\mathbb R have relative degree r with respect to the control input u. Define the auxiliary functions

\psi_0=h,\qquad \psi_i=\dot\psi_{i-1}+\alpha_i(\psi_{i-1}),\quad i=1,\dots,r,

where the \alpha_i are class-\mathcal K functions. The admissible set is

\mathcal C=\bigcap_{i=0}^{r}\{x:\psi_i(x)\ge 0\}.

Then h is a high-order CBF if there exists u such that \psi_r(x,u)\ge 0.

G.2 Invariance Theorem

Theorem. If h is a HOCBF of relative degree r and the initial state satisfies \psi_i(x_0)\ge 0 for all i, then any Lipschitz controller satisfying \psi_r(x,u)\ge 0 renders \mathcal C forward invariant.

Proof sketch. The comparison lemma applied to \dot\psi_{i-1}\ge -\alpha_i(\psi_{i-1}) recursively shows \psi_{i-1}(t)\ge 0 for all t, and in particular \psi_0=h\ge 0. ∎

G.3 Application to the Gap Constraint

With r=3 (due to actuator lag), h=g-g_{\min}:

\psi_0=h,\qquad \psi_1=\dot h+k_1h,\qquad \psi_2=\ddot h+k_2\dot h+k_3\psi_1,

and \psi_3=h^{(3)}+k_4\ddot h+k_5\dot h+k_6h\ge 0. The k_i are chosen so that the polynomial

s^3+k_4s^2+k_5s+k_6

is Hurwitz with bandwidth \omega_n<1/(\tau_e+\tau_F).

Design values. With \omega_n=20 rad/s and \zeta=0.9: k_4=3\zeta\omega_n=54, k_5=3\omega_n^2+3\zeta^2\omega_n^2\approx 2280, k_6=\omega_n^3=8000.

G.4 Compatibility with Actuator Box

The set

\mathcal U(x)=\{u\in[\![-F^-_{\max},F^+_{\max}]\!]:\psi_3(x,u)\ge 0\}

is non-empty provided the required \dot F_{\rm active} for HOCBF satisfaction lies within the actuator slew rate limit. This is encoded as an additional constraint in the QP.

