Appendix H: Clarke CBF for Nonsmooth Gear Contact

H.1 Clarke Generalised Gradient

For a locally Lipschitz h:\mathbb R^n\to\mathbb R, the Clarke gradient is

\partial_C h(x)=\operatorname{conv}\bigl\{\lim_{k\to\infty}\nabla h(x_k):x_k\to x,\ h\text{ differentiable at }x_k\bigr\}.

H.2 Clarke CBF Definition

A locally Lipschitz h is a Clarke CBF if for every x\in\{h=0\} and every \xi\in\partial_C h(x), there exists u such that

\xi\cdot(f(x)+g(x)u)>0.

H.3 Invariance

Theorem. If h is a Clarke CBF, then \{h\ge 0\} is forward invariant under any admissible control.

Proof. By the chain rule for Clarke gradients (F. H. Clarke, Optimization and Nonsmooth Analysis, Thm 2.3.9), h(x(t)) is absolutely continuous and

\frac{d}{dt}h(x(t))\in\xi\cdot\dot x(t),\qquad \forall\xi\in\partial_C h(x(t)).

If h(x(t))=0 at some t, then by assumption there exists u making \dot h>0, so h cannot cross below zero. Continuity of trajectories gives invariance. ∎

H.4 Application to h_G=F_G-F_{G,\min}

The gear force F_G=\sigma f_G^{\rm HC}(z_{\rm rel},\dot z_{\rm rel}) is Lipschitz but not C^1 at z_{\rm rel}=0. The Clarke gradient is the convex hull of the gradients computed in the interior and exterior of the contact region. The QP handles the resulting set-valued constraint by enforcing the linear inequality for all \xi in the Clarke gradient.

---

Appendix I: ISSf-CBF for Power Constraint

I.1 Definition

A differentiable h is an input-to-state-safe CBF for the system \dot x=f(x)+g(x)u+d if there exist a class-\mathcal K_\infty function \gamma and a class-\mathcal K function \iota such that

\dot h\ge-\gamma(h)+\iota(\|d\|).

I.2 Application

For h_D=P_{\max}-P_D(v,g):

\dot h_D=-\frac{\partial P_D}{\partial v}\dot v-\frac{\partial P_D}{\partial g}\dot g
=-\frac{\partial P_D}{\partial v}\frac{1}{m}\bigl(F_{\rm thrust}+d_z\bigr)-\frac{\partial P_D}{\partial g}\dot g.

The disturbance d_z appears affinely. Choosing \gamma(h)=\alpha_0 h and \iota(s)=s, the QP adds

\alpha_0 h_D-\frac{\partial P_D}{\partial v}\frac{u_{\rm thrust}}{m}-\frac{\partial P_D}{\partial g}\dot g\ge\|d_z\|_{\infty},

which is affine in u_{\rm thrust}. ∎

---

Appendix J: Hunt–Crossley Contact Model Properties

J.1 Model

f_G^{\rm HC}=k_G z^n\Bigl(1+\frac{3(1-e)}{2}\frac{\dot z}{\dot z^{(-)}}\Bigr),\qquad z\ge 0,

and f_G^{\rm HC}=0 for z<0.

J.2 Zero Force at Touchdown

At first contact z=0, f_G^{\rm HC}=k_G\cdot 0\cdot(\cdots)=0. ✓

J.3 Force Continuity

The function is continuous in (z,\dot z) on the contact region and matches f_G=0 at z=0^+. This removes the discontinuity that the linear model exhibits.

J.4 Energy Dissipation

The hysteretic loop area over a loading-unloading cycle is

\Delta E=\int_{\rm cycle}f_G^{\rm HC}\,dz=\frac{3(1-e)}{2}\cdot\frac{1}{\dot z^{(-)}}\int k_G z^n\dot z\,dz.

For e=1 the model reduces to elastic Hertzian contact with no dissipation; for e<1 it produces the standard restitution ratio e.

J.5 Parameter Selection

For the reference gear, choose n=1.5 (Hertzian), k_G=2\times 10^5 N/m^{1.5}, e=0.3. Then the static compression to support mg is

z_{\rm static}=\bigl(mg/k_G\bigr)^{2/3}=\bigl(4905/2\times 10^5\bigr)^{2/3}\approx 0.011\ \text{m}=11\ \text{mm}.

This is small compared to the gear stroke s_G=150 mm.

---

Appendix K: Existence of the Hybrid Discriminating Kernel

K.1 Hybrid Dynamics

The state (x,q)\in\mathbb R^5\times\{0,1,2,3\} evolves by

\dot x=f_q(x)+g_q(x)u+d_q(t),\qquad q^+=\text{Jump}(q,x,u,\text{fault}).

Faults enter as (i) additional disturbances d_q, and (ii) forced jumps to a "failed" mode.

K.2 Assumptions

1. f_q,g_q are locally Lipschitz for each q.
2. \mathcal K,\mathcal C_{\rm gear} are closed.
3. The boundary of \mathcal K admits a viability tangential condition for each q.
4. The jump map is outer semicontinuous.

K.3 Existence Theorem (Aubin–Saint-Pierre adapted)

Theorem. Under these assumptions, the hybrid discriminating kernel

\operatorname{Disc}(\mathcal K,\mathcal C_{\rm gear})=\bigl\{x_0:\exists u(\cdot),\forall d(\cdot),\forall\text{fault timing},\exists T,\ x(T)\in\mathcal C_{\rm gear},\ x(t)\in\mathcal K\ \forall t\in[0,T]\bigr\}

is closed and non-empty whenever \mathcal C_{\rm gear}\ne\varnothing and the tangential condition holds.

Proof. Apply the Saint-Pierre fixed-point theorem on the set-valued map F:\mathcal K\rightrightarrows\mathcal K defined by viable hybrid trajectories, using the Kuratowski–Ryll-Nardzewski selection theorem for the control and the viability theorem for differential inclusions (Aubin–Cellina) for the disturbance. ∎

K.4 Grid Approximation Direction

Inner approximation: \operatorname{Disc}_N\subseteq\operatorname{Disc}. Any x_0\in\operatorname{Disc}_N can be driven to \mathcal C_{\rm gear}, so the safety claim holds on the computed set. The dual statement (viability kernel outer approximation) is used only for detecting failure.

---

Appendix L: Saint-Pierre Algorithm Convergence

L.1 Set-Valued Map

Define

F(\mathcal A)=\{x\in\mathcal A:\exists u,\forall d,\ x+\int_0^{\Delta t}(f+gu+d)\in\mathcal A\}.

The kernel is the greatest fixed point \mathcal A^*=F(\mathcal A^*).

L.2 Convergence

The algorithm iterates \mathcal A_{k+1}=F(\mathcal A_k). Under Lipschitz dynamics and closed constraint set:

1. \mathcal A_k is decreasing: \mathcal A_{k+1}\subseteq\mathcal A_k.
2. Convergence in the Hausdorff metric holds in O(1/N) for grid size N.
3. The limit is contained in \operatorname{Disc}.

L.3 Hybrid Case

For the hybrid system, the algorithm is applied recursively over modes. The convergence rate is O(1/N) uniformly in the mode index provided the jump map is Lipschitz in the continuous state.

---

Appendix M: Feasibility Compatibility (Lemma 6.1)

Let u_{\rm nom} strictly satisfy all constraints with margin \delta>0:

g_i(u_{\rm nom},x)\ge\delta,\qquad \forall i.

Each g_i is continuous in (u,x). There exists \epsilon>0 such that for \|u-u_{\rm nom}\|<\epsilon and \|x-x_{\rm nom}\|<\epsilon,

g_i(u,x)\ge\delta/2>0.

The feasible set is therefore non-empty in a neighbourhood. The nominal QP solution inherits this property by the projection argument. ∎

---

Appendix N: Non-Emptiness of \mathcal D_H (Theorem 8.1)

N.1 Necessary Condition

The hybrid support condition requires

F_{\rm PMEDS}(v,g)+F^+_{\rm active,max}\ge mg

or equivalently

F_\infty(g)\ge \bigl(mg-F^+_{\rm active,max}\bigr)\bigl(1+(v_t/v)^2\bigr).

At v\to\infty this reduces to F_\infty(g)\ge mg-F^+_{\rm active,max}. Since F_\infty is strictly decreasing in g, the set

\mathcal G_+=\{g\in[g_{\min},g_{\max}]:F_\infty(g)\ge mg-F^+_{\rm active,max}\}

is either empty or a closed interval [g_{\min},g_+].

N.2 Sufficient Condition

Pick g\in\mathcal G_+ with F_\infty(g)>mg-F^+_{\rm active,max} (strict). Then there exists a velocity v_{\rm hyb}(g)<\infty such that the support condition holds for all v>v_{\rm hyb}(g).

The power constraint P_D(v,g)\le P_{\max} holds for all v if P_D^\infty(g)=F_\infty(g)v_t\le P_{\max}, i.e. F_\infty(g)\le P_{\max}/v_t.

The deployment-timing constraint is satisfied for v sufficiently larger than v_{\rm phys}^{\sup}.

Intersecting the three open sets gives a non-empty open subset of \mathcal D_H. ∎

N.3 Numerical Check

At g=20 mm: F_\infty=8707 N, mg=4905 N, mg-F^+_{\rm active,max}=4905-3000=1905 N (using F^+_{\rm active,max}=3 kN). Since 8707>1905, the support condition is satisfiable. The power boundary P_D^\infty=13.95 kW <P_{\max}=20 kW. The deployment-timing constraint is satisfiable for v\ge 4 m/s with t_{\rm deploy}=0.5 s. Hence \mathcal D_H\neq\varnothing. ✓

N.4 Downward Authority Requirement

For sustained cruise at v=8 m/s, g=20 mm, the passive lift is F_{\rm PMEDS}=8383 N. The required downward active force for vertical equilibrium is

F_{\rm active}=mg-F_{\rm PMEDS}=4905-8383=-3478\ \text{N}.

At v=30 m/s, g=15 mm: F_{\rm PMEDS}\approx 11920 N, F_{\rm active}=4905-11920=-7015 N.

Therefore

\boxed{F^-_{\rm active,max}\ge 7015\ \text{N}\approx 7.0\ \text{kN}}.

With margin, the reference design uses F^-_{\rm active,max}=8 kN.

