Appendix O: Numerical Derivations for the Reference Vehicle

O.1 Force Constants

B_0=B_r(1-e^{-kd})\sin(\pi/M)/(\pi/M).

With B_r=1.4 T, d=0.02 m, k=31.4 m^{-1}, M=4: 1-e^{-0.628}=0.466, \sin(\pi/4)/(\pi/4)=0.900. Thus B_0=1.4\cdot 0.466\cdot 0.900=0.587 T. (Note: the reference uses 0.8 T, requiring stronger magnets.)

For B_0=0.8 T (design target):

F_\infty(0)=\frac{0.64\cdot 0.25\cdot 1.2}{2\cdot 31.4\cdot 2\times 10^{-6}\cdot 0.05}=\frac{0.192}{6.28\times 10^{-6}}=30573\ \text{N}.

At g=0.020 m: e^{-2\cdot 31.4\cdot 0.020}=e^{-1.256}=0.285, so F_\infty(0.020)=8707 N.

O.2 Physical Levitation Velocity

v_{\rm phys}(g)=\frac{v_t}{\sqrt{F_\infty(g)/mg-1}},\qquad v_t=1.6\ \text{m/s},\ mg=4905\ \text{N}.

· g=15 mm: F_\infty=11920 N, ratio =2.43, \sqrt{1.43}=1.196, v_{\rm phys}=1.34 m/s.
· g=20 mm: F_\infty=8707 N, ratio =1.775, \sqrt{0.775}=0.880, v_{\rm phys}=1.82 m/s.
· g=25 mm: F_\infty=6364 N, ratio =1.297, \sqrt{0.297}=0.545, v_{\rm phys}=2.94 m/s.

O.3 Drag Power at Reference Speeds

P_D(v,g)=F_\infty(g)v_t\frac{v^2}{v^2+v_t^2}.

· v=8, g=20 mm: P_D=8707\cdot 1.6\cdot 64/(64+2.56)=8707\cdot 1.6\cdot 0.961=13.4 kW.
· v=30, g=15 mm: P_D=11920\cdot 1.6\cdot 900/(900+2.56)=11920\cdot 1.6\cdot 0.997=19.0 kW.

At v=30 m/s the power approaches the 20 kW budget, so the design operating envelope is bounded by power at high speed.

O.4 Propulsion Power at Cruise

P_{\rm prop}^{\rm cruise}(v)=v\bigl(\tfrac12\rho C_dA v^2\bigr).

For C_dA=0.7 m^2, \rho=1.225 kg/m^3, v=8 m/s: P_{\rm prop}=8\cdot 27=216 W. At v=30 m/s: P_{\rm prop}=30\cdot\tfrac12\cdot 1.225\cdot 0.7\cdot 900=30\cdot 386=11.6 kW.

O.5 Total Power at Cruise

At v=8 m/s: P_{\rm total}=13.4+0.2+P_{\rm ctrl}\approx 15.6 kW.

At v=30 m/s: P_{\rm total}=19.0+11.6+P_{\rm ctrl}\approx 32.6 kW. This exceeds the 20 kW budget. Therefore v_{\max}=30 m/s is infeasible under the reduced-order model; the design maximum speed is bounded by P_{\rm total}(v_{\max})=P_{\max}, giving v_{\max}\approx 22 m/s.

O.6 Minimal Damping Bound

k_d^{\min}\approx 1.69\times 10^4\ \text{N·s/m}.

At maximum gap rate \dot g_{\max}=1 m/s, this requires active force authority

F_{\rm active}^{\rm damp}=k_d^{\min}\dot z_{\max}\approx 17\ \text{kN}.

This exceeds the 8 kN static authority and constrains the closed-loop bandwidth. The design trade-off: use k_d\approx 5\times 10^3 N·s/m with reduced \dot z_{\max} or increase F^+_{\rm active,max}.

O.7 Closed-Loop Bandwidth

\omega_{\rm cl}=\sqrt{K/m}\sqrt{1+2k_d\tau_e/m}\approx 32\sqrt{1+2\cdot 5000\cdot 0.02/500}=32\sqrt{1.4}=37.9\ \text{rad/s}.

ECBF design bandwidth \omega_n=20 rad/s provides margin.

O.8 Hunt–Crossley Static Compression

With k_G=2\times 10^5, n=1.5: z_{\rm static}=(4905/2\times 10^5)^{2/3}=0.011 m.

Landing at \dot z^{(-)}=1 m/s, e=0.3:

Peak force f_G^{\rm HC}\approx k_Gz^{1.5}(1+1.05)\approx 2\times 10^5\cdot(0.05)^{1.5}\cdot 2.05=2\times 10^5\cdot 0.0112\cdot 2.05\approx 4.6 kN.

Landing energy J_{\rm landing}=\int F_G\,dt\approx\tfrac12 m\dot z^2=250 J for \dot z=1 m/s.

