Appendix P: Hybrid Automaton Well-Posedness

P.1 Mode Guards

Define the guard conditions:

\mathcal G_{0\to 1}=\{v\ge v_{\rm engage}\}\cap\{\text{corridor}\}\cap\{F_{\rm PMEDS}+F_{\rm active}\ge mg\},

\mathcal G_{1\to 2}=\{v\ge v_{\rm rob}\}\cap\{g\in[g_{\min},g_{\max}]\}\cap\{\sigma=0\},

\mathcal G_{2\to 1}=\{v<v_{\rm rob}\}\cup\{\sigma>0\}\cup\{\text{fault}\},

\mathcal G_{1\to 0}=\{v<v_{\rm disengage}\}\cup\{\text{fault}\}.

P.2 Existence and Uniqueness of Executions

Proposition. Under the assumption that the guards \mathcal G_{i\to j} are closed subsets of the state space, and that no two guards are simultaneously active except at isolated points, the hybrid automaton admits a unique maximal execution for every initial condition.

Proof. Standard existence for hybrid automata (Lygeros, Johansson, Sastry). The uniqueness follows from the priority ordering of the guards and the Lipschitz vector fields. ∎

P.3 Zeno Behaviour

The automaton is Zeno-free if there exists a minimum dwell time \tau_{\rm dwell}>0 between any two transitions. The guards are designed so that \tau_{\rm dwell}\ge t_{\rm deploy}\approx 0.5 s, which prevents chattering.

